# ui-properties.mdc

## Property design

Prefer:

- module-level constants
- typed getters/setters
- centralized configuration
- documented defaults

Avoid:

- magic numbers
- duplicated preference keys
- hard-coded strings repeated in multiple files

# New properties — report at end of task

When you add or change **tunable properties** (styling constants, widget flags, preference keys, menu actions, getters/setters), finish the work with a short **Properties** section in your final message to the user.

## What counts

- Module constants used for look-and-feel (e.g. `_HEADER_TEXT`, `_AXIS_HEADER_PX`)
- Widget state (`_show_axis_labels`, `set_show_axis_labels`, `show_axis_labels()`)
- Persisted prefs (`display.block_tooltips`, `display.grid_axis_labels` in `config/editor_settings.yaml` / user YAML)
- View/menu wiring (`_grid_axis_labels_action`, `_apply_*_pref`)

## End-of-task summary format

Use a compact table or bullet list: **name**, **where** (file or key), **role**, **default** if relevant.

Example (axis labels):

| Property | Location | Role |
| -------- | -------- | ---- |
| `_HEADER_TEXT` | `ui/widgets/grid.py` | Header label color |
| `_show_axis_labels` | `LayerGridWidget` | Show column numbers / row letters |
| `display.grid_axis_labels` | `config/editor_settings.yaml` | Saved View-menu preference |

Do **not** skip this summary when the change introduced new properties the user may want to find or tweak later — even if the main task was something else (feature, bugfix, refactor).

## Code placement

Keep constants with related code (typically top of module, near the widget). “End of process” means **end of your response**, not necessarily end of the file.
